Forkspace creates disposable project workspaces using the fastest backend the host supports.

Linux overlayfs, APFS clones, Linux reflinks, and a portable copy floor share one command and state contract. Backend selection is automatic and sticky per workspace.

Usage: forkspace <COMMAND>

Commands:
  new          Create a new workspace from a directory or another workspace
  drop         Remove a workspace and unmount its overlay
  enter        Open a shell (or run a command) inside a workspace
  run          Start a process in a workspace (dev server, Electron, etc.)
  switch       Switch the active workspace (hot-swap via proxy)
  ls           List all active workspaces
  diff         Show what changed in a workspace
  compare      Compare parallel workspace attempts side by side
  merge        Apply workspace changes back to the base directory
  pull         Bring base changes into a workspace
  land         Preserve a workspace's Git branch in the base repository
  checkpoint   Save the current state of a workspace
  checkpoints  List a workspace's checkpoints
  restore      Return a workspace to one of its checkpoints
  limit        Show or change a workspace's memory and CPU limits
  gc           Show what disk forkspace could give back, and reclaim it
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
